<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fin de siècle</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as fin-de-siècle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fin de siècle ('end of the century') refers to the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, a period associated with cultural innovation and a sense of decadence. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>, it saw a flowering in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psychoanalysis">psychoanalysis</a>, philosophy, painting, architecture and music.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fin-de-siecle.jpg" alt="Fin de siècle" width="320" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Achille Lemot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bilan_fin_de_si%C3%A8cle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Fin de siècle is a French term meaning end of century. It covers the sense of the English phrase turn of the century and also the closing of one era and the beginning of another, and without context it usually means the end of the nineteenth century. The period was widely thought to be one of social degeneracy and at the same time of hope for a new beginning. The cultural hallmarks of the 1880s and 1890s included ennui, cynicism, pessimism and a widespread belief that civilisation leads to decadence. The term is commonly applied to French art and artists, since the traits first appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, but the movement affected many European countries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>B. A. Morel's degeneration theory held that societies can regress as well as progress under a flawed environment, with the effects passed from generation to generation. In Degeneration Max Nordau held that the two dominant traits of the degenerate were ego mania and mysticism, and his work brought the concept of genius back into popular thought. Pessimism spread from the work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-schopenhauer">Arthur Schopenhauer</a>, and R. H. Goodale counted 235 essays by British and American authors on pessimism between 1871 and 1900. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscar-wilde">Oscar Wilde</a> joked in An Ideal Husband that optimist and pessimist were the only fashionable religions left.</p><p>Decadent writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-baudelaire">Charles Baudelaire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-rimbaud">Arthur Rimbaud</a> influenced their English counterparts, the aesthetes, who held that art should evoke an emotional response and show beauty in the unnatural. Holbrook Jackson listed the characteristics of English decadence as perversity, artificiality, egoism and curiosity. Gothic fiction expressed fears of change and decay through the human body, in works such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-louis-stevenson">Robert Louis Stevenson</a>'s The Strange Case of Dr Jekyll and Mr Hyde (1886), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscar-wilde">Oscar Wilde</a>'s The Picture of Dorian Gray (1891), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/h-g-wells">H. G. Wells</a>'s The Time Machine (1895) and Bram Stoker's Dracula (1897). The age also produced a flood of future war narratives, sold in tens of thousands of copies across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, which acted as political advocacy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The ideas of fin de siècle artists gave the impetus for symbolism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modernism">modernism</a>. The political culture of the time was marked by revolt against materialism, rationalism, positivism, bourgeois society and liberal democracy, and favoured emotionalism, irrationalism and vitalism. Its themes were controversial and have been cited as a major influence on fascism and on the beginnings of geopolitics, including the theory of Lebensraum. Michael Heffernan has argued that geopolitics arose in fin de siècle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from technological change and the sense that imperialist competition had closed the world's frontiers. Writing of the 1890s, he notes a widespread assumption that the passing of the century would mean a clear break with the past.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fin de siècle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Vienna's <b>fin-de-siècle</b> culture rested on this prosperity: the Ringstrasse, the Secession movement, coffee-house intellectual life and the professional class that produced Sigmund Freud, Gustav Mahler, Gustav Klimt and Theodor Herzl all belonged to a prosperous, cosmopolitan urban economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
